    "text": "To find the minimum value of the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\), we start by applying the AM-GM (Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean) inequality. The AM-GM inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers \\(a, b, c\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b + c}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{abc}.\n\\]\n\nWe will apply this inequality to the terms in the numerator of our expression. Let's consider the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\). We can rewrite the numerator as \\(x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3\\). To apply AM-GM, we need to express these terms in a form that allows us to use the inequality. We can write:\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3 = x^3 + y^3 + y^3 + y^3 + z^3 + z^3 + z^3 + z^3.\n\\]\n\nNow, applying the AM-GM inequality to these eight terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + y^3 + y^3 + y^3 + z^3 + z^3 + z^3 + z^3}{8} \\geq \\sqrt[8]{x^3 \\cdot y^3 \\cdot y^3 \\cdot y^3 \\cdot z^3 \\cdot z^3 \\cdot z^3 \\cdot z^3} = \\sqrt[8]{x^3 \\cdot y^{12} \\cdot z^{12}} = \\sqrt[8]{x^3 y^{12} z^{12}} = (x^3 y^{12} z^{12})^{1/8} = (x^3 y^{12} z^{12})^{1/8} = (x^{3/8} y^{12/8} z^{12/8}) = (x^{3/8} y^{3/2} z^{3/2}) = (x^{3/8} (yz)^{3/2}).\n\\]\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to directly apply AM-GM to the terms \\(x^3, 2y^3, 4z^3\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{x^3 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot 4z^3} = \\sqrt[3]{8x^3 y^3 z^3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot xyz = 2\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot xyz.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3 \\geq 3 \\cdot 2\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot xyz = 6\\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot xyz.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} \\geq 6\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n\\]\n\nTo check if this minimum value can be achieved, we need to find \\(x, y, z\\) such that the equality holds in the AM-GM inequality. The equality in AM-GM holds when all the terms are equal, so we set \\(x^3 = 2y^3 = 4z^3\\). Let \\(x^3 = k\\), then \\(2y^3 = k\\) and \\(4z^3 = k\\). This gives us \\(y^3 = \\frac{k}{2}\\) and \\(z^3 = \\frac{k}{4}\\). Therefore, \\(y = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{2}}\\) and \\(z = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{4}}\\).\n\nSubstituting these into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} = \\frac{k + k + k}{k \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k}{4}}} = \\frac{3k}{k \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{k^2}{8}}} = \\frac{3k}{k \\cdot \\frac{k^{2/3}}{2^{1/3}}} = \\frac{3k \\cdot 2^{1/3}}{k^{5/3}} = 3 \\cdot 2^{1/3} = 6\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value is achieved and is equal to \\(6\\sqrt[3]{2}\\). Therefore, the minimum value is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n\\]",
